  "solution": "### 1. Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**\n\nI have successfully solved the problem. The dimension of the orbit $Y_{pm}$ is given by the formula:\n$$ \\dim Y_{pm} = \\sum_{i \\in I_{pm}} i + \\sum_{j \\in J_{pm}} (n-j+1) - N_{pm} $$\nwhere:\n*   $I_{pm}$ is the set of row indices appearing in the partial matching $pm$, i.e., $I_{pm} = \\{i \\mid \\exists j, (i,j) \\in pm\\}$.\n*   $J_{pm}$ is the set of column indices appearing in $pm$, i.e., $J_{pm} = \\{j \\mid \\exists i, (i,j) \\in pm\\}$.\n*   $N_{pm}$ is the number of pairs of elements in $pm$ that are comparable under the product order on indices, i.e.,\n    $N_{pm} = |\\{((i,j), (i',j')) \\in pm \\times pm \\mid i \\le i' \\text{ and } j \\le j'\\}|$.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\nThe solution proceeds as follows:\n1.  The problem is set over the complex numbers $\\mathbb{C}$. The dimension of the orbit $Y_{pm}$ is calculated using the orbit-stabilizer theorem for Lie group actions: $\\dim Y_{pm} = \\dim G - \\dim \\text{Stab}_G(A_{pm})$, where $G = B_m \\times B_n$.\n2.  The dimension of the stabilizer subgroup is equal to the dimension of its Lie algebra, $\\mathfrak{s} = \\mathfrak{stab}_{\\mathfrak{g}}(A_{pm})$. The Lie algebra of $G$ is $\\mathfrak{g} = \\mathfrak{b}_m \\times \\mathfrak{b}_n$, where $\\mathfrak{b}_k$ is the Lie algebra of $k \\times k$ upper triangular matrices. An element $(X_1, X_2) \\in \\mathfrak{g}$ belongs to $\\mathfrak{s}$ if and only if it satisfies the linear equation $X_1 A_{pm} = A_{pm} X_2$.\n3.  The core of the proof is to establish a direct sum decomposition of the stabilizer Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{s}$. We prove the following lemma:\n\n    **Lemma:** The stabilizer Lie algebra decomposes as $\\mathfrak{s} = V_1 \\oplus V_2 \\oplus V_3$, where:\n    *   $V_1 = \\{(X_1, 0) \\in \\mathfrak{g} \\mid \\text{columns of } X_1 \\text{ with indices in } I_{pm} \\text{ are zero}\\}$.\n    *   $V_2 = \\{(0, X_2) \\in \\mathfrak{g} \\mid \\text{rows of } X_2 \\text{ with indices in } J_{pm} \\text{ are zero}\\}$.\n    *   $V_3 = \\text{span}\\{(E_{i, i'}^{(m)}, E_{j, j'}^{(n)}) \\mid ((i,j), (i',j')) \\in pm \\times pm, i \\le i', j \\le j' \\}$, where $E_{ab}^{(k)}$ is the $k \\times k$ matrix unit.\n\n4.  The proof of this decomposition involves showing that any element of $\\mathfrak{s}$ can be uniquely written as a sum of elements from these three subspaces. This is done by explicitly constructing the projection of an arbitrary element of $\\mathfrak{s}$ onto each subspace.\n5.  The dimension of $\\mathfrak{s}$ is then the sum of the dimensions of these subspaces, which are calculated by counting free parameters:\n    *   $\\dim V_1 = \\sum_{k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}} k$.\n    *   $\\dim V_2 = \\sum_{k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}} (n-k+1)$.\n    *   $\\dim V_3 = N_{pm}$.\n    This gives $\\dim \\mathfrak{s} = \\sum_{k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}} k + \\sum_{k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}} (n-k+1) + N_{pm}$.\n6.  The dimension of the orbit is then found by substituting this result into the orbit-stabilizer formula and simplifying, using the fact that $\\dim B_k = \\frac{k(k+1)}{2} = \\sum_{i=1}^k i$.\n\n### 2. Detailed Solution ###\n\nWe consider the space of matrices $X = M(m,n; \\mathbb{C})$ and the action of the Lie group $G = B_m \\times B_n$, where $B_k \\subset GL_k(\\mathbb{C})$ is the Borel subgroup of invertible upper triangular matrices. The dimension of an orbit $Y_{pm} = G \\cdot A_{pm}$ is given by the orbit-stabilizer theorem:\n$$ \\dim Y_{pm} = \\dim G - \\dim \\text{Stab}_G(A_{pm}) $$\nThe dimension of the Lie group $G$ is the sum of the dimensions of its components:\n$$ \\dim G = \\dim B_m + \\dim B_n = \\frac{m(m+1)}{2} + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} $$\nThe dimension of the stabilizer subgroup $\\text{Stab}_G(A_{pm})$ is equal to the dimension of its Lie algebra, $\\mathfrak{s} = \\mathfrak{stab}_{\\mathfrak{g}}(A_{pm})$. The Lie algebra of $G$ is $\\mathfrak{g} = \\mathfrak{b}_m \\times \\mathfrak{b}_n$, where $\\mathfrak{b}_k$ is the Lie algebra of all $k \\times k$ upper triangular matrices. An element $(X_1, X_2) \\in \\mathfrak{g}$ is in $\\mathfrak{s}$ if it satisfies the condition $X_1 A_{pm} - A_{pm} X_2 = 0$, or\n$$ X_1 A_{pm} = A_{pm} X_2 $$\nLet $pm = \\{(i_p, j_p) \\mid p=1, \\dots, r\\}$, where $r = |pm|$. Let $I_{pm} = \\{i_1, \\dots, i_r\\}$ and $J_{pm} = \\{j_1, \\dots, j_r\\}$. Let $X_1 = (x_{ik}) \\in \\mathfrak{b}_m$ and $X_2 = (y_{lj}) \\in \\mathfrak{b}_n$. The $(a,b)$-th entry of the matrix equation is:\n$$ \\sum_{k=1}^m x_{ak} (A_{pm})_{kb} = \\sum_{l=1}^n (A_{pm})_{al} y_{lb} \\implies \\sum_{p=1}^r x_{a, i_p} \\delta_{j_p, b} = \\sum_{p=1}^r \\delta_{a, i_p} y_{j_p, b} $$\nThis equation yields the following constraints on the entries of $X_1$ and $X_2$:\n(C1) $x_{i_p, i_q} = y_{j_p, j_q}$ for all $p,q \\in \\{1, \\dots, r\\}$.\n(C2) $x_{a, i_q} = 0$ for all $q \\in \\{1, \\dots, r\\}$, $a \\notin I_{pm}$, and $a \\le i_q$.\n(C3) $y_{j_p, b} = 0$ for all $p \\in \\{1, \\dots, r\\}$, $b \\notin J_{pm}$, and $j_p \\le b$.\n\nTo compute $\\dim \\mathfrak{s}$, we establish a direct sum decomposition of this vector space. Let us define three subspaces of $\\mathfrak{g}$:\n*   $V_1 = \\{(X_1, 0) \\in \\mathfrak{g} \\mid (X_1)_{ab} = 0 \\text{ for all } b \\in I_{pm}\\}$.\n*   $V_2 = \\{(0, X_2) \\in \\mathfrak{g} \\mid (X_2)_{cd} = 0 \\text{ for all } c \\in J_{pm}\\}$.\n*   $V_3 = \\text{span}\\{(E_{i_p, i_q}^{(m)}, E_{j_p, j_q}^{(n)}) \\mid p,q \\in \\{1, \\dots, r\\}, i_p \\le i_q, j_p \\le j_q\\}$, where $E_{ab}^{(k)}$ is the $k \\times k$ matrix unit with a 1 at position $(a,b)$ and 0 elsewhere.\n\n**Lemma:** $\\mathfrak{s} = V_1 \\oplus V_2 \\oplus V_3$.\n\n**Proof of Lemma:**\nFirst, we show that $V_1, V_2, V_3 \\subset \\mathfrak{s}$.\n*   For $(X_1, 0) \\in V_1$: Since $X_2=0$, (C3) is satisfied. For any $i_q \\in I_{pm}$, the $i_q$-th column of $X_1$ is zero, so $x_{i_p, i_q}=0$ and $x_{a, i_q}=0$. This satisfies (C1) (as $0=0$) and (C2). Thus $V_1 \\subset \\mathfrak{s}$.\n*   For $(0, X_2) \\in V_2$: Since $X_1=0$, (C2) is satisfied. For any $j_p \\in J_{pm}$, the $j_p$-th row of $X_2$ is zero, so $y_{j_p, j_q}=0$ and $y_{j_p, b}=0$. This satisfies (C1) (as $0=0$) and (C3). Thus $V_2 \\subset \\mathfrak{s}$.\n*   For a basis element $(E_{i_p, i_q}^{(m)}, E_{j_p, j_q}^{(n)})$ of $V_3$: (C1) $x_{i_k, i_l} = \\delta_{k,p}\\delta_{l,q}$ and $y_{j_k, j_l} = \\delta_{k,p}\\delta_{l,q}$, so they are equal. (C2) $x_{a, i_l} = \\delta_{a, i_p}\\delta_{l,q}$. This is non-zero only if $a=i_p \\in I_{pm}$. The condition applies only when $a \\notin I_{pm}$, so it holds. (C3) $y_{j_k, b} = \\delta_{k,p}\\delta_{b, j_q}$. This is non-zero only if $b=j_q \\in J_{pm}$. The condition applies only when $b \\notin J_{pm}$, so it holds. Thus, the basis elements of $V_3$ are in $\\mathfrak{s}$, which implies $V_3 \\subset \\mathfrak{s}$.\nFrom these inclusions, we have $V_1 + V_2 + V_3 \\subset \\mathfrak{s}$.\n\nNext, we show that any element $(X_1, X_2) \\in \\mathfrak{s}$ has a unique decomposition into a sum of elements from $V_1, V_2, V_3$.\nLet $(X_1, X_2) \\in \\mathfrak{s}$. Define:\n*   $v_1 = (X_1', 0)$, where $(X_1')_{ab} = x_{ab}$ if $b \\notin I_{pm}$ and $0$ otherwise. By definition, $v_1 \\in V_1$.\n*   $v_2 = (0, X_2')$, where $(X_2')_{cd} = y_{cd}$ if $c \\notin J_{pm}$ and $0$ otherwise. By definition, $v_2 \\in V_2$.\n*   $v_3 = (X_1'', X_2'') = (X_1, X_2) - v_1 - v_2 = (X_1-X_1', X_2-X_2')$.\n\nSince $\\mathfrak{s}$ is a vector space and $(X_1, X_2), v_1, v_2 \\in \\mathfrak{s}$, their linear combination $v_3$ must also be in $\\mathfrak{s}$.\nBy construction, $(X_1'')_{ab} = 0$ if $b \\notin I_{pm}$, and $(X_2'')_{cd} = 0$ if $c \\notin J_{pm}$.\nSince $v_3 \\in \\mathfrak{s}$, it satisfies (C1-C3).\n(C2) for $v_3$ implies $x''_{a, i_q} = 0$ for $a \\notin I_{pm}$. Combined with $(X_1'')_{ab} = 0$ for $b \\notin I_{pm}$, this means the only non-zero entries of $X_1''$ can be at positions $(i_p, i_q)$.\n(C3) for $v_3$ implies $y''_{j_p, b} = 0$ for $b \\notin J_{pm}$. Combined with $(X_2'')_{cd} = 0$ for $c \\notin J_{pm}$, this means the only non-zero entries of $X_2''$ can be at positions $(j_p, j_q)$.\n(C1) for $v_3$ implies $x''_{i_p, i_q} = y''_{j_p, j_q}$.\nSince $X_1''$ and $X_2''$ are upper triangular, $x''_{i_p, i_q}$ can be non-zero only if $i_p \\le i_q$, and $y''_{j_p, j_q}$ only if $j_p \\le j_q$. If one of these inequality conditions fails, both entries must be zero.\nLet $c_{pq} = x''_{i_p, i_q}$ for pairs $(p,q)$ where $i_p \\le i_q$ and $j_p \\le j_q$. Then $v_3 = (X_1'', X_2'') = \\sum_{p,q \\text{ s.t. } i_p \\le i_q, j_p \\le j_q} c_{pq} (E_{i_p, i_q}^{(m)}, E_{j_p, j_q}^{(n)})$, which shows $v_3 \\in V_3$.\nThe decomposition $(X_1, X_2) = v_1 + v_2 + v_3$ is unique by construction. This proves $\\mathfrak{s} = V_1 \\oplus V_2 \\oplus V_3$. $\\blacksquare$\n\nNow, we compute the dimensions of these subspaces.\n*   $\\dim V_1$: The non-zero entries of $X_1$ can only be in columns $k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}$. For each such column $k$, there are $k$ entries $(a,k)$ with $a \\le k$. So, $\\dim V_1 = \\sum_{k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}} k$.\n*   $\\dim V_2$: The non-zero entries of $X_2$ can only be in rows $k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}$. For each such row $k$, there are $n-k+1$ entries $(k,d)$ with $k \\le d$. So, $\\dim V_2 = \\sum_{k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}} (n-k+1)$.\n*   $\\dim V_3$: The basis vectors are in one-to-one correspondence with pairs of pairs $((i_p, j_p), (i_q, j_q)) \\in pm \\times pm$ such that $i_p \\le i_q$ and $j_p \\le j_q$. The number of such pairs is $N_{pm}$. So, $\\dim V_3 = N_{pm}$.\n\nThe dimension of the stabilizer's Lie algebra is:\n$$ \\dim \\mathfrak{s} = \\dim V_1 + \\dim V_2 + \\dim V_3 = \\sum_{k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}} k + \\sum_{k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}} (n-k+1) + N_{pm} $$\nFinally, we compute the dimension of the orbit:\n$$ \\dim Y_{pm} = \\dim G - \\dim \\mathfrak{s} $$\n$$ \\dim Y_{pm} = \\left(\\frac{m(m+1)}{2} + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\right) - \\left(\\sum_{k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}} k + \\sum_{k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}} (n-k+1) + N_{pm}\\right) $$\nUsing $\\frac{m(m+1)}{2} = \\sum_{k=1}^m k$ and $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} = \\sum_{k=1}^n (n-k+1)$, we get:\n$$ \\dim Y_{pm} = \\left(\\sum_{k=1}^m k - \\sum_{k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}} k\\right) + \\left(\\sum_{k=1}^n (n-k+1) - \\sum_{k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}} (n-k+1)\\right) - N_{pm} $$\nSince $I_{pm}$ and $[m]\\setminus I_{pm}$ partition $[m]$, and $J_{pm}$ and $[n]\\setminus J_{pm}$ partition $[n]$, the sums simplify:\n$$ \\sum_{k=1}^m k - \\sum_{k \\in [m]\\setminus I_{pm}} k = \\sum_{k \\in I_{pm}} k $$\n$$ \\sum_{k=1}^n (n-k+1) - \\sum_{k \\in [n]\\setminus J_{pm}} (n-k+1) = \\sum_{k \\in J_{pm}} (n-k+1) $$\nSubstituting these back gives the final formula for the dimension of the orbit:\n$$ \\dim Y_{pm} = \\sum_{i \\in I_{pm}} i + \\sum_{j \\in J_{pm}} (n-j+1) - N_{pm} $$",
